# What is LMR/lemur, why does it exist?

.lmr is short for lemur and originally had a longer explanation for its name,
but essentially it stands for a light markup language that is inspired primarily
by reStructuredText, Markdown, and LaTeX. I developed it simply because I mostly
like reStructuredText except for the headers, and started writing in a 'reduced'
variant of it. I also wanted to have a bit more flexibility in expressing
tables, in-line math, etc. that goes beyond Markdown. Using LaTeX all the way
was also too exhausting. I thus set out to find a minimal set of things that
make me happy and, importantly, that fit into my head. 

Also, lemurs are cute.


## Version, Stability, Language Specification ^version

Version 0.0.3

What? This sounds very unstable! -- Yes, lmr is young. It evolves along my
daily use, so I wouldn't call it particularly stable yet. That said, the things
declared in this document are what I use most of the time and have settled on.
I'm open for suggestions, as long as they are lightweight but allow some
flexibility.

This document is the specification: there is no separate standard beyond it.
lemur is developed jointly with its reference implementation, and the text below
refers to its two halves. The __parser__ reads lemur and produces a neutral
document tree (the AST, whose exact shape is given by `spec/ast.schema.json`).
An __emitter__ turns that tree into something to look at -- for instance a
slide deck. The parser decides what a document __means__; an emitter decides how
it __looks__, so the language never says "pixel" or "font".


## Design Philosophy ^philosophy

The design philosophy behind lemur is rather simple: It needs to fit into my
head, and ideally uses keys that I have easily available on a keyboard. Also, I
rarely start a new line with `!` or `::`, so let's exploit this.


## Comments ^comments

A line whose first non-whitespace characters are `%%` is a comment and will be
ignored entirely. I rarely start a line with `%%`, and it is close enough to
LaTeX's `%` to feel familiar. Comments work almost everywhere, including
between the rows of a table or between the lines of a paragraph (the paragraph
simply continues). The one exception are the bodies of code blocks: they are
verbatim, so a `%%` in there stays what it is. This is on purpose, because
`%%` is meaningful in languages such as Erlang or MATLAB.

If a line in running text really needs to start with a literal `%%`, escape it
as `\%%`.


## Indentation ^indentation

Indentation in lemur is structural, but lemur does not fix a tab width and
does not care whether you indent with tabs or spaces. Instead, the first line
of a block body defines the indentation of that block: its exact leading
whitespace must appear at the start of every following line of the same block.
Deeper indentation on top of that prefix is content and is preserved, which is
what you want for nested code. A line that is indented but does not start with
the block's prefix is an error, since it is almost certainly a typo.

This means you can use a tab in one block and three spaces in the next, and
nobody has to win a "4 vs 8" debate. It also means that a code snippet should
start at its shallowest line; a block whose first line is deeper than a later
line is rejected.


## Special Keywords ^keywords

The following are not really special keywords of lmr, but I use them often
enough that an editor's syntax highlighting for lemur should make them stand
out.

FIXME
