NOTE


## How to use tables ^tables

Tables are declared by using a `!table` statement which needs to be the first on
a line (except preceding whitespace), followed by an optional human readable
title of the table, followed by an optional reference (see @references further
below).

That means that the following lines all declare a new table

:: lemur

	!table
	!table human readable table name
	!table some title ^table_ref
	!table ^tab_ref

The first line simply tells that there's a table.
The second line declares a table with some table name
The third line declares a table with a human readable name and a reference
The fourth line declares a table without a title, but with a reference

The next part of declaring a table is in specifying columns and their names.
This also includes specifying column separators, and optionally an alignment
attribute for each column. Examples might help:

:: lemur

	!cols First, Second, Third 
	!cols First, Second; Third | Fourth
	!cols First[l], Second[r] | Third[c]
	!cols "Long header, with comma and escaped \" quotes"[c], "Second[yadda]"[r] 

The first row introduces three columns, separated by `,`.
The second row introduces four columns with separators `,`, `;`, and `|`.
The third line adds alignment indicators in the square brackets, which are one
of `l` for left alignment, `r` for right alignment, and `c` for center
alignment.

The separator is the author's choice: __any__ punctuation character works, not
just `,`, `;` and `|` — `!cols A & B & C` or `!cols A / B / C` are equally
valid. Only alphanumerics and spaces count as part of a name, so a header that
itself contains punctuation (a comma, an `&`, parentheses, a hyphen, …) must be
surrounded by `""`.

The fourth line shows exactly this: lmr lets you surround a column name with
`""`.  Thereby it's possible to use separators (and any other punctuation)
within a column name, and it's easier to handle whitespace, or square brackets.
To use `"` within such a header definition, simply escape it using the `\`
symbol, i.e. `\"` as on the fourth line.

Then, there's also the possibility to add a caption to a table. This is done via

:: lemur

	!caption Here goes a long caption...

You can also rule off groups of rows: a line of `===` draws a strong separator
and a line of `---` a light one, in between the data rows.

To drop the heading row entirely, add `!noheader` to the table. The `!cols`
declaration is still required -- it defines the columns, their separators and
their alignment -- but its names are not rendered. Give bare alignments (or
empty names) when you have nothing to head the columns with:

:: lemur

	!table
	!cols [l], [r]
	!noheader

		Alice , 30
		Bob   , 25

A cell may also span multiple lines: write `\n` wherever you want a manual line
break. It works in any prose run too, and a literal backslash-n is written
`\\n`.

:: lemur

	!cols Name, Notes

		Alice , first line \n second line

A note on how rows are split: the separators declared in `!cols` are searched
in the same order within each data row. This is exactly why the separator is
the author's choice: pick separators that do not appear inside your cells. If
a cell does contain one (a comma in prose, a `|` in math), the row mis-splits,
and the interpreter warns whenever the number of cells in a row does not match
the number of declared columns.

Putting all together, the following is an example of table.

:: lemur

	!table my-table ^table_ref
	!cols Name, Age[r], Country[c] | "something else"
	!caption Here goes a longer, optional caption of the table. This can span
	multiple lines, but the multiple lines are not allowed to contain empty lines in
	between. Otherwise it'll require more syntax, which I try to avoid.

		Alice   , 30 , USA      | yes 
		===
		Bob     , 25 , Canada   | no
		Charlie , 35 , UK       | yes
		---
		Steve   , 27 , Denmark  | yes


## Inline Math ^inline_math

I need math, often in some inline form, so we simply adopt LaTeX inline math
with a single `$`: $\sum_{i=1}^{10}{i^2}$. This way, math from existing LaTeX
documents can be copied over for free. The price is that a literal dollar sign
in running text must be escaped as `\$`, e.g. "this costs \$5". Inside math
mode, `\$` stays what it is in TeX.

`$$` is not a lemur construct. The parser warns when it encounters `$$` or an
odd number of unescaped `$` on a line, which catches most forgotten escapes.
Note that an even number of unescaped currency dollars ("between $5 and $6")
cannot be detected in principle and will be read as math, so escape your
currencies.

For longer math, use a [display math block]@display_math.


## Display Math ^display_math

A `:: math` block holds display math: its indented body is LaTeX, exactly what
you would put between `\[` and `\]`.

:: lemur

	:: math ^bayes
		p(\theta \mid x) = \frac{p(x \mid \theta)\, p(\theta)}{p(x)}

Several rows go in one block: write `\\` between rows and `&` at the alignment
points, and the rows are aligned. The top-level LaTeX display environments
(`align`, `gather`, `equation`, ...) may also be pasted in as they are.
Equation numbering is not part of lemur; give a block a `^ref` to point at it.

Parts of an equation can be named with `\mk{name}{...}` so they can be
coloured and pointed at (see @highlight).


## Formatting ^formatting

`this` was a code example.

Some text where **this** is bold, __this__ is italic, ~~this~~ is struck
through, and ++this++ is underlined.

Bold italic: **__bold italic__**
Italic bold: __**italic bold**__

Four doubled markers, chosen so nothing in ordinary prose triggers by accident:
`**bold**`, `__italic__`, `~~strikethrough~~`, `++underline++`. Each also has a
style-class fallback — `.bold`/`.italic`/`.strike`/`.underline`, which are handy
when composing with other attributes (`[x]{.underline #c00}`). (Underline
collides with `C++`, so you have to code-span it: `` `C++` `` or use an escape
such as C\++)

Smart typography. On every prose run (never inside `code`, `$math$`, or a URL —
those are lifted out first), straight ASCII is educated to real Unicode: `--`
becomes an en dash (ranges: pages 3--5), `---` an em dash (asides---like this),
`...` an ellipsis, and straight quotes/apostrophes curl by context ("quotes",
don't, the '90s).

Escaping. A backslash forces the next special character literal — `\"` a
straight quote, `\--` a plain double hyphen, and likewise `\*`, `` \` ``, `\@`,
`\[`, `\$`. (A `\` before an ordinary character is just a backslash.) A `code`
span is never touched either, so `--flag` inside backticks also stays verbatim.

Styling. Beyond `**`/`__`/`~~`, a span can carry a `{…}` **style attribute** with
one rule used *everywhere*: `.name` is a named style (a built-in like `.bold`/
`.italic`/`.underline`/`.strike`/`.center`, or your own `.yeehaa` that a theme
or template defines), `#rrggbb` (or a css colour name) is a foreground colour,
and `bg:#eef` a background. So `[word]{.bold #c0392b}` is
bold and red, `[word]{.accent}` uses a themed class. It composes with the other
span suffixes — `[key]{.accent .bold}^m<2->` is a styled, named mark that
appears at step 2. This is deliberately **not** a formatting language: a span
carries only style names and colour hints, and the emitter decides how they
actually look.

Styled blocks. `!style[.class #color bg:…]` is the block counterpart — an
indentation-delimited container (like `!columns`) that styles its whole body.
Built in are `.center` (centre the content) and `.frame` (a rounded border — a
box); `bg:` tints the background; other `.class` names are the theme's to define:

::
	!style[.center .frame bg:#f5f7ff]
		A centred, framed panel on a tinted background.

		$E = mc^2$

"Built-in" means that every emitter understands these names; any other class is
for a theme or template to define.


## Code Blocks ^code_blocks

A code block is started by `::`, followed by an indented block of code. This is
mostly identical to how `.. code-block::` works in rST, just shorter. If no
language is specified after `::`, then this could be styled simply in monospace
font. But, you can also indicate which language this by using `:: lang`, where
lang corresponds to some language. for instance `:: cpp` means there's a block
of C++ code coming.

In addition, you can attach references to language blocks just as you can attach
references anywhere else. For instance `:: cpp ^arena_alloc_ref` creates a C++
block with a reference named `arena_alloc_ref`.

Here are some examples:

:: sh
	#!/bin/sh

	echo "hello world"


:: ^s
	code block without any language specification


:: ^coderef
	code block with code reference


:: tex ^tex_code_block

	\section{Hello World}

	$\sum_{i=1}^{10}{i^2}$


:: cpp

	void main() {
		std::string s = "1234";
		this is still cpp
		return 0;
	}


:: python ^pyref

	s = "1234"
	def main():
		print("Hello, World")


::

	@article{
		author = {one, two},
		journal = {journal of something}
	}


## Images

Images are kept separate from the running text, much like in LaTeX and like the
`!table` statement above. An `!img` block takes an optional title and `^ref`,
then an indented body of directives — of which only `!src` (where the image
comes from) is mandatory:

::
	!img Some image title ^img_ref
	!src https://some.tld/img.png
	!caption A funny lemur in front of a wonderful landscape.
	!width 80%

`!src` is a URL or a path relative to the document, so the minimal image is just:

::
	!img
	!src some/relative/path.png

`!width` and `!height` size the image -- a percentage is a fraction of the
text column, like LaTeX's `\includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]` -- and
`!caption` adds a caption line. Several `!src` lines stack as layers that reveal one at a
time (see steps, below) in slides: `!mode overlay` (the default) overprints each
new layer on the previous, while `!mode replace` swaps each for the one before,
as in the example below:

::
	!img
	!src path/to/img0.png
	!src path/to/img1.png
	!src path/to/img2.png
	!caption This is the caption for this image
	!mode replace


## References, links, and citations ^references

References are introduced with `^` and used with `@`, inspired by pointers in
the Pascal programming language, where `^` declares a pointer and `@`
dereferences it. Attach `^name` to anything referable — a slide, heading, table,
code block, image, or environment (`!slide Intro ^intro`, `:: cpp ^alloc`) — and
point back to it later with `@name`, which links to the target and shows its
label (its number or title), much like LaTeX's `\ref{}`. Give it your own
wording with `[text]@name`.

A reference/citation name is letters, digits, `_` and `-` (the same class as a
mark), so any other punctuation *terminates* it: `@jonas2010:` cites `jonas2010`
and leaves the colon as text, and `@jonas2010, @pearl1988.` both resolve. A `@`
may even abut a preceding word — `Kording@jonas2010` is a citation — while a real
email (`me@host.tld`, with a dotted domain) is left alone. For a key that needs
odd characters, use the explicit `@(key)` form, whose parentheses delimit it.

Links and emails. A bare URL or email in running text is left as-is (not
linked). To make one a link, wrap it in angle brackets —
`<https://rochus.net>`, `<rochus@rochus.net>` — which shows the URL or address
itself, or give it your own wording with `[text]@(https://…)`.

Citations. When a name resolves to a literature entry (see below), `@key`
renders as a citation. `@(key1, key2)` cites several at once, and
`[text]@(key1, key2)` replaces the marker with your own wording — like
`\cite{…}` in LaTeX.


## Literature

A bibliography entry is a definition line, `^key: <entry text>`; each becomes a
numbered, referable entry:

^doe2025:   John Doe, 2025, Journal of Does, Article Title, Publisher.
^alice2024: Alice, A. 2024, Another Journal, Another Title, Pub2.


## Link targets

A named link target uses the same form with an angle-bracketed URL,
`^name: <url>`. It produces no visible output of its own — it only gives `@name`
something to point at:

^website: <https://rochus.net>
^mail:    <rochus@rochus.net>


## Slides and presentations ^slides

The lemur language contains a thin slide layer on top of the language
above. These constructs are layouting commands and not the core markup, but are
documented here so the language and its main use stay in one place. The parser
records them in the AST like everything else, and a slide emitter turns them
into slides.

Structure. `!slide Title ^ref` starts a slide; `# Title ^ref` is a section
divider; `##`/`###` inside a slide are headings. `!notes` (indented body) is
speaker notes, kept in the source but not shown.

Per-slide style. `!slide[.name] Title` (or `#[.name] Title`) adds a style class to
that slide, so a theme or template restyles just that slide. Built in: `.plain`
(hide the chrome), `.fill` (drop the content padding), and body alignment
`.center` (horizontal) and `.middle` (vertical) — these centre the *body* below
the slide title, leaving the title and chrome in place. The option bracket
attaches with no space right after the directive/marker, and holds one or more
`.name` classes (`!slide[.center .middle] Title`). Because it must touch the
directive, a title may itself start with `[`.

Steps (build-up). Everything is driven by one integer step per slide. Step 0 is
the slide as first shown (no keypress); each keypress advances the step (1, 2,
…). `!pause` makes all following content appear together on the next keypress. A
`+` list item is incremental (one keypress each); `-`/`*` and `1.` are static.
Extra `!src` layers of an `!img` reveal one at a time, and so do the
highlight groups of a code block and the lines of `!annotate` and `!connect`.
The beats of an animation and the steps of a shader (see @computed) follow once
everything else on the slide has been revealed. A slide deck addresses every moment of a talk as
`#/<slide>/<step>`.

Lists. Bullets are `-` or `*` (static) and `+` (incremental); ordered items are
`N.` (the numbers are recomputed — just write `1.`). Nest by indenting a
sub-list under its parent item; each level is its own list. A running step
counter flows through the items in reading order — `+` advances it, `-`/`*`/`N.`
sit at the current value — so an item inherits the step of the previous thing
shown, and a *nested* item inherits its parent's step (a sub-list under a `+`
item appears with that item, not from the start).

Overlay control. For explicit control of when things show — and hide again —
attach an overlay spec `<spec>`: `+<2->`/`-<3>` on a list item, `[content]<2->`
on an inline run so a word or phrase appears mid-sentence without being a bullet
(a named mark takes it too: `[content]^name<2->`, so the mark and its connector
reveal together), or a `!when<spec>` **block** to gate several blocks at once.
`!when` is indentation-delimited like `!columns`/a table: everything indented
under it is gated by the spec, and a dedent ends it. `!when` blocks nest, and
nesting narrows (the inner spec applies inside). A spec is a comma-separated list
of `n` (only n), `n-` (from n), `-n` (up to n), or `n-m` (a range); an open-ended
`n-` stays, a bounded spec can hide again. Hidden runs reserve their space, so
revealing one never reflows the line. `+`/`!pause` are just the common `<n->` case.

Relative steps. To avoid writing step numbers (which are cumbersome and don't
survive copy-paste), use `+` inside the spec: `<+->` shows from the next
auto-allocated step onward (like a `+` item, but for any content), and `<+>`
shows only during it. They compose everywhere — `[phrase]<+->`, `!when<+->`,
`-<+>` — and the parser numbers them in document order, so you can reorder or
copy freely.

::
	Reveal [this]<+-> and then [this]<+->, no numbers needed.

	!when<+->
		A whole block on the following step.

::
	!when<2->
		Both of these appear on keypress 2.

		:: python
			and_so_does = this_code_block

Spacing. `!gap` adds vertical space between blocks: bare for a default gap,
`!gap[2em]` for a fixed length, or `!gap[fill]` to grow and push the content
below it to the bottom (it turns the slide body into a distributing column).

Stacked overlays. `!stack` starts a block whose `!layer<spec>` children overlap
in one spot, so a layer takes the place of the previous one instead of stacking
vertically; the box reserves the tallest layer, so content below it never moves.
Every `!layer` needs an explicit overlay `<spec>` in angle brackets — the same
`<…>` bracket as `!when` and `+<…>` — with no default. Like `!columns`, the block
is indentation-delimited and a dedent ends it.

Columns. `!columns[60 40 .boxed]` starts side-by-side columns; its body is
indented and each `!column`'s content is indented under it in turn (like a table
or code block), so a dedent ends things — no end marker. In the bracket a numeric
token is a relative column width (any number of columns; omit for equal) and
everything else is a style attribute for the container (`.boxed` etc.). Each
`!column[.center]` takes the same attribute; the built-in `.center`/`.bottom`
classes set its vertical alignment (`.top` is the default). The step counter
flows across columns, so a `!pause` in one column defers the content after it.

Nesting. Columns, stacks and environments nest freely — each is just an
indentation-delimited container: a `!stack` inside a `!column`, a `!columns`
inside a `!layer` or an environment, columns inside columns, and so on. Only a
`!column`/`!layer` may hold content directly inside a `!columns`/`!stack` (a
stray line there is an error). A handy pattern is an outer `!stack` whose layers
each carry a whole column layout, so a keypress swaps the layout:

:: lemur

	!stack
		!layer<1>
			!columns[50 50]
				!column
					first layout, left
				!column
					right
		!layer<2->
			!columns[30 70]
				!column
					second layout, a wider right
				!column
					right

Code line highlighting. `:: lang[1|4-6|3,7]` steps through line-highlight
groups (each `|`-group is one keypress). The bracket attaches to the language
with no space. A language colorizes the code.

Configuration (before the first slide): `!title`, `!subtitle`, `!author`,
`!institute`, `!date`, `!titleimage`, `!logo <path>`, `!header <text>`,
`!footer <text>`, `!slidenumbers <on|off>`, `!progress [top|bottom]` (a thin
progress bar along that edge), `!theme <name>`,
`!transition <slide> [<step>]` (across-slide `none|fade|slide|push` — `slide`
moves the whole slide, `push` only the content so the chrome stays; within-slide
step `none|fade|rise`), `!aspect <16:9|4:3>`. The
header/footer are shown on content slides; how they look (a thin line, a
full-bleed brand bar with the logo, ...) is up to the theme.

Files. `!include path.lmr` on a line of its own inserts another file at that
point, as if its text were written there; the path is relative to the including
file. Includes nest, and an include cycle is an error. A typical talk is a
`master.lmr` with the configuration and one `!include` per chapter.


## Highlighting and annotation ^highlight

Parts of an equation — or of running text — can be named and then coloured and
pointed at, without ever writing a coordinate.

Naming. A *mark* tags a piece of content with a name. In math, write
`\mk{name}{content}` (`$\mk{sum}{\sum_i x_i}$` or inside a `:: math` block); in
prose, write `[content]^name` (`the [important part]^key`). `name` is letters,
digits, `_`, `-`. Reusing a name on the same slide **groups** the occurrences:
they share one colour, one annotation label's arrow fans out to each, and a
`!connect` pairs each with its nearest counterpart — handy when the same quantity
appears in a compact form and again, expanded, later in the equation. (An
accidental reuse only warns, since it is usually a typo.)

Annotating. `!annotate` (indented body) attaches one `name[color]: label`
line per named part:

::
	!annotate
		sum[#3a7d44]: marginalize out everything except $x$
		fac: weigh by local factor

Each line is its own step: on that keypress the named part changes colour, the
short label appears, and a curved arrow is drawn from the label to the part — one
arrow per occurrence when the name is a group, all on the same step. The emitter
places label and arrow from the typeset layout, so nothing is positioned by
hand and edits to the content never break an arrow. The `[…]` bracket attaches to the name with no
space and carries the usual style vocabulary — a colour and dotted style names
(`.bold`/`.italic`/`.underline`), e.g. `key[#c00 .bold]:`. The colour is optional
— `[#rrggbb]` or a css colour name; otherwise a palette cycles. A `name[color]:`
line with no label is a colour-only step. Colours accumulate, so successive steps
build up the equation. Keep labels
short; they do not wrap.


## Connecting marks ^connect

Where `!annotate` points a *generated label* at a mark, `!connect` draws an
arrow *between two marks* — turn the arrow around to explain, or link a question
to its answer. Both marks are just named content (defined with `[…]^name` in
prose or `\mk{name}{…}` in math); they can sit anywhere, including on the same
line.

`!connect` (indented body) has one connector per line, `from <arrow> to`
followed by an optional option bracket:

::
	What is [computed]^q1? And how is it [feasible]^q2?

	The answers: [theory of computation]^a1 and [compositional models]^a2.

	!connect
		q1 -> a1  [#c0392b]
		q2 -> a2  [#2e5e6e .dashed]

The arrow glyph gives direction: `->` (from→to), `<-` (to→from), `<->` (both
ends), `--` (a plain line, no head). The option bracket holds a colour
(`#rrggbb` or a css name) and/or line-style keywords (`dashed`, `thin`, `thick`,
written bare or dotted). Each line is its own step, cumulative, like an annotate
line; the connector attaches to the facing edges of the two marks, placed from
the typeset layout like an annotation arrow. A mark
that is absent on the slide simply draws no arrow. If either name is a group
(used more than once), the connector covers every occurrence of the larger side,
pairing each with its nearest counterpart.


## Semantic environments ^environments

For scientific and technical writing, lemur knows a fixed set of *semantic
environments* — `!theorem`, `!lemma`, `!corollary`, `!proposition`,
`!definition`, `!proof`, `!example`, `!remark`, `!intuition`, `!note`,
`!warning`, `!exercise`, `!historical`. Each is a block with an optional title
(and optional `^ref`) and an indentation-delimited body, like `!columns`:

::
	!theorem Fixed-point on a tree ^bp-fix
		On a tree, sum-product converges to the exact marginals in a finite
		number of steps.

	!proof
		A leaf-to-root then root-to-leaf sweep stabilises every message.

The body may hold *any* block — math, tables, code, lists — and environments
**nest** (a `!remark` inside a `!proof`, an `!intuition` inside an `!example`);
a dedent ends the innermost one. Each renders as a titled box that a theme can
restyle per kind. There is deliberately *no* way to define new environments
(that macro system is out of scope), and numbering is left to the theme.


## Computed figures ^computed

Three blocks put figures on a slide that are __computed__ rather than drawn: a
plot, an animation, and a live shader. Each takes an optional title and `^ref`,
like `!img`, followed by directives -- of which `!src`, the program that makes
the figure, is mandatory. The program is a file next to the document; it is
trusted code and runs on the author's machine (or, for a shader, on the
viewer's graphics card).

Plots. `!plot` runs a Python script that draws a figure with matplotlib and
places the result like an image, with `!caption`, `!width` and `!height`:

::
	!plot Posterior over time ^post
		!src posterior.py
		!caption The posterior sharpens as data arrives.
		!width 70%

The script hands over its figure as a `figure()` or `plot()` function, a
module-level `fig`, or simply as the current pyplot figure.

Animations. `!anim` runs a Python animation written with the `lemur.anim`
library: a class derived from `Anim` whose `build()` plays animations on shapes,
text and maths, in 2-D or seen through a 3-D camera. Each `self.next()` in
`build()` marks a __beat__, and every beat is one step of the slide, so the
animation advances with the rest of the slide's build-up. `!viewport` says where
it is drawn: `body` (the slide body, the default), `full` (the whole slide), or
`x y w h`, a rectangle in the design's coordinates.

::
	!anim ^hello
		!src hello.py
		!viewport body

:: python
	from lemur.anim import Anim, Circle, Create, RIGHT

	class Hello(Anim):
		def build(self):
			c = Circle()
			self.play(Create(c))
			self.next()                  # one slide step
			self.play(c.animate.shift(RIGHT * 2))

Shaders. `!shader` runs a GLSL fragment shader live, while the slide is shown.
The shader follows the convention of Shadertoy -- a function
`mainImage(out vec4 color, in vec2 pixel)` with the uniforms `iResolution`,
`iTime`, `iTimeDelta`, `iFrame` and `iMouse` -- so shaders from there can be
pasted in. lemur adds the slide's step: `!steps N` gives the shader `N` steps of
its own, which it reads as `iStep` (eased, so the scene glides from one state to
the next), `iStepRaw` (the whole number) and `iSteps`. `!viewport` works as for
`!anim`; `full` draws the shader behind the slide's text. `!quality` (a factor
up to 1) caps the resolution, and `!sound drone` or `!sound aurora` attaches a
generative soundtrack that stays silent until the presenter switches it on. A
line `#include "file.glsl"` in the shader inserts another file, resolved when the
document is built.

::
	!shader
		!src terrain.glsl
		!viewport full
		!steps 3
		!sound drone
